    "created_at": "2026-11-18T00:40:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-18T00:40:26Z",
    "body": "Important detail from the reporter: \"crashes\" means **the whole computer**: the picture goes wonky with \"snow\", then the PC locks up and needs a hard reset. That is a GPU driver or hardware hang (integrated Radeon on a desktop Ryzen 5), not an app crash - an app alone cannot freeze Windows. CamCraft's GPU load triggers it.\n\nConsequences for this issue:\n- A log file will usually NOT survive a hard lock, so logging alone will not diagnose this case. Writing the adapter/driver line at startup (flushed to disk before the first frame) still helps: the last run's log would name the GPU path that froze.\n- What would help this user directly: a **safe graphics mode** - start on the software adapter (wgpu's fallback adapter / WARP on dx12) with a flag such as `--gpu-backend software`, and offer it on the next start when the previous run did not exit cleanly. Worth its own issue if the maintainer agrees.\n\nKnown causes of \"snow then lock-up\" on Ryzen APUs to rule out first: an old AMD driver; memory overclock (XMP/EXPO) - the APU uses system RAM as video memory, so unstable RAM shows up as screen corruption under GPU load; an old BIOS; heat.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "pin": null,
